<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/13th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Babai revolt</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1239–<a href="/years/1240">1240</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Babai rebellion</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Babai revolt was a rising of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> against the Seljuk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultanate-of-rum">Sultanate of Rum</a> in 1239–1240, inspired by the preachers Baba Ilyas and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baba-ishaq">Baba Ishaq</a>. Fuelled by religious fervour and resentment of heavy taxes and the Persianised court, the rebels defeated several Seljuk forces before being crushed with Frankish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a>. The revolt weakened the sultanate shortly before the Mongol victory at Köse Dağ in 1243.</p></div>
